Maintaining the opencode package
================================

Read this before a version bump. The package is unusual in three ways: the
sources are generated rather than downloaded, the whole npm dependency tree is
compiled into one executable, and three of the five patches exist only to stop
the program reaching the network at runtime. None of that is obvious from the
spec alone.


Bumping the version
-------------------

    ./opencode_vendor 1.18.30 --retrace

That one command produces every source the spec consumes:

    opencode-<version>.tar.zst          upstream source, slimmed
    opencode-vendor-<version>.tar.zst   the npm dependency tree
    models.dev-api-<version>.json       the model catalogue snapshot

It needs bun, zstd, curl and - for --retrace - strace, and it goes to the
network. Drop --retrace only if you already know the dependency closure has
not moved; see "The keep list" below for what it costs to get that wrong.

Then, in order:

1. Check the @opentui/core pin. The spec has a hard equality check in %prep,
   so a moved pin fails the build with an explanatory message rather than
   producing something subtly broken - see "OpenTUI" below. If it moved, bump
   the opentui package first and update opentui_version here.

2. Rebase the five patches. They are small and all of them sit in code
   upstream changes rarely, but the grammar patch in particular is a single
   line in a large file. opencode-native-tree-sitter.patch replaces the
   parser() block of packages/opencode/src/tool/shell.ts; if upstream
   changes what shell.ts reads from a node, shell-native.ts (added by the
   patch) must grow with it.

3. Check the native pins. %prep fails if the vendor tree's @ff-labs/fff-bun,
   bun-pty, @silvia-odwyer/photon-node or @parcel/watcher moved away from
   fff_version, bun_pty_version, photon_version or parcel_watcher_version;
   bump the fff, bun-pty and photon-node packages first (their ABIs are
   private to the TypeScript in the tree, so the Requires are exact). The
   grammar modules follow the tree-sitter-<lang>-wasm packages: the
   BuildRequires are exact on ts_*_version, so a tree-sitter-<lang>
   update in Factory makes this package unresolvable until the pin (and
   the bundled() version it feeds) follows - by design. On an opentui
   bump re-check its parsers-config.ts pins and highlight queries
   against those versions. See "Native code" below.

4. Build locally, then read %check. It runs the compiled binary, which is the
   only thing that proves the payload survived packaging.

5. Re-run the licence check described under "Licensing".


Why the sources are generated
-----------------------------

opencode is built by `bun build --compile`, which needs the complete npm
dependency tree present on disk. There is no way to build it from a release
tarball, and OBS has no network during a build, so the tree has to be a
source.

The upstream tarball is not shipped as-is either. The repository holds the
web console, the desktop app and the marketing site alongside the CLI: about
48 MB of mp4, plus the Inter and JetBrains Mono fonts, which come with no
licence text at all. None of it is reachable from the CLI build.
opencode_vendor keeps the thirteen workspace packages the build reads and
drops the rest, taking Source0 from 78 MB to 7.4 MB.

`--skip-embed-web-ui` is what makes that possible: the browser interface
would pull in another 101 npm packages and embed those two fonts.

Upstream also patches 18 of its dependencies, listed under
patchedDependencies in the root package.json and applied by bun during the
install. What ends up in the executable is therefore the patched code, and it
is the dependency tarball that carries it - the package build never runs an
install. The patches/ directory is shipped in Source0 all the same, because
it is the only readable record of how third-party code inside the binary was
changed, and because a package.json naming files that are not there is not
something a reviewer should have to work out.


The keep list
-------------

opencode-vendor-keep.txt names the 511 store entries the vendor tarball
keeps, out of the 1000 that are left once the filtered install has had the
platforms we do not build for removed. It is derived, not curated.
`--retrace` runs the real build under strace, resolves every path it touched
back to the package that owns it, prunes to that set and rebuilds to prove
the result still links. It applies opencode-relax-bun-version.patch so the
trace can run under a bun that is not the one upstream pins, then reverses
it so Source0 stays the upstream tree and Patch0 still applies at %prep.

The install runs with `--cpu '*' --os '*'`. Bun 1.4 resolves
optionalDependencies for the host platform alone, so an install done on
aarch64 contains no @opentui/core-linux-x64, @ff-labs/fff-bin-linux-x64-gnu
or @parcel/watcher-linux-x64-glibc. A plain run notices - the script's keep
list check stops with "keep list names packages the install did not produce"
- but --retrace is what the list is regenerated with, and the counterpart
step described below can only add packages the install produced. Without
these flags it writes a 508-entry list instead of 511 and the x86_64 build
on OBS, which is not the architecture this is generated on, is what finds
out. The step after the install prunes the platforms we really do not want
back out.

Two things a trace cannot see, both handled by opencode_vendor_trace:

  - bun opens most modules through the per-workspace symlink farms
    (packages/opencode/node_modules/...), not through node_modules/.bun, so
    the paths are resolved before they are attributed;

  - the trace runs on one architecture, so every package that exists only for
    the other one looks unused. Their counterparts are added back by name.

Getting the list wrong cannot ship a broken binary. A missing module is a
hard resolution error from bun, and OBS builds both architectures. It can
only fail the build.

The pruning was verified content-neutral when it was introduced: the binary
built from the pruned tree was byte-for-byte the same size, and the only
differing strings were chunk filenames and embedded build paths.


OpenTUI
-------

opencode renders its interface with OpenTUI, which is TypeScript plus a
native libopentui.so talking over a private FFI ABI with no versioning of its
own. The two halves must come from the same release, so opencode has to be
built against exactly the version it pins in the catalog block of the
top-level package.json. %prep checks that and fails loudly if it moved.

The dependency tree still uses npm's @opentui/core TypeScript. The native
libopentui.so is not copied into the tree and is not compiled into the
binary: %prep rewrites the @opentui/core-linux-* loader to export the path
under %{_libdir}/opentui that the opentui package ships, and the RPM
Requires: opentui at that same version. Swapping the whole @opentui/core
package for the distro one does not work (npm ships built JS with assets/;
the RPM ships source-tree exports). Only the .so is the compiled half, and
it is loaded at runtime via bun:ffi.


Runtime network access
----------------------

Upstream opencode reaches the network on its own initiative in several ways
that are not appropriate for a distribution package. Three patches change
that. Each restores upstream behaviour through an environment variable, so
nothing is taken away permanently, and each patch's header records what it
found and why the behaviour could not simply be configured off.

  opencode-no-self-update.patch
        One second after start it asks GitHub for the latest release and, if
        it recognises how it was installed, overwrites its own executable.
        OPENCODE_ENABLE_AUTOUPDATE=1

        Note that "autoupdate": false in /etc/opencode/opencode.json does
        NOT disable this, which is easy to get wrong. upgrade() reads
        cfg.getGlobal(), which loads only ~/.config/opencode; the /etc merge
        happens in loadInstanceState, a different path.

  opencode-no-runtime-npm-install.patch
        It installs npm packages into the user's home: @opencode-ai/plugin on
        every start, unprompted, plus formatters, language servers and
        provider SDKs on demand. Npm.reify is the single choke point.
        OPENCODE_ALLOW_NPM_INSTALL=1

  opencode-no-grammar-download.patch
        All 34 syntax highlighting grammars in
        packages/tui/src/parsers-config.ts are https URLs; the .wasm comes
        from a GitHub release and the highlight queries from the master
        branch of nvim-treesitter, so what gets fetched changes over time.
        The five grammars @opentui/core ships as real assets - javascript,
        typescript, markdown, markdown_inline, zig - are not in that table
        and still work; other filetypes render unhighlighted, which is what
        upstream already does when a download fails.
        OPENCODE_ALLOW_GRAMMAR_DOWNLOAD=1

31 of the 34 languages that paragraph is about now come from the
distribution instead: %prep rewrites packages/tui/src/parsers-config.ts so
that each descriptor's wasm and highlights point at
/usr/share/tree-sitter/wasm/tree-sitter-<grammar>.wasm and
/usr/share/tree-sitter/queries/<grammar>/highlights.scm (the loader takes a
local path exactly as it takes a URL, and a missing file means plain text
for that language, nothing worse), and the package Recommends the
tree-sitter-<lang>-wasm and -queries pair for each of them
(opencode_system_grammars in the spec; csharp's grammar is c_sharp in
tree-sitter-c-sharp; cpp's descriptor lists the C highlights before its
own, as upstream's tree-sitter-cpp query set is a delta on top of them).
The rewrite also drops the locals entries: nothing in @opentui/core reads
them, and they would otherwise keep web URLs in descriptors the download
block no longer filters. Three upstream entries stay blocked on purpose:
hcl, whose packaged grammar ships no queries; kotlin, where Factory
packages another grammar than the fwcd one upstream pins, also without
queries; and vue, whose highlights.scm inherits html_tags (upstream lists
two sources, the rewrite handles exactly one) and ends in a #set! with a
capture argument that web-tree-sitter 0.25 rejects, so it would never
compile. The rewrite asserts exactly one upstream highlights source and no
injections or locals left per descriptor; a descriptor that grows another
kind of entry needs the rewrite extended first.
%check loads every listed module with its queries in the web-tree-sitter
runtime opencode ships, so a query naming a node the packaged grammar lacks
fails the build rather than the highlighter. On a bump, diff upstream's
descriptor list against opencode_system_grammars: a new language needs its
grammar packaged (with -wasm and -queries) before it is added; until then
Patch3 keeps it blocked.

The remaining patch is unrelated to the network.

opencode-relax-bun-version.patch is, as of 1.18.32, inert. Upstream's build
script fails unless bun satisfies a caret range around the version in the
packageManager field: 1.18.32 pins bun@1.3.14 and Factory has 1.4.2, which
satisfies ^1.3.14, so the check passes and the patch changes nothing. Keep it
anyway. Both versions float independently, a distribution ships one bun, and
the day either side crosses a major the check would stop a build for a reason
that is upstream's convenience rather than a real incompatibility - the build
itself is Bun.build() plus shell calls, and %check runs the resulting binary,
so a bun that really is too new fails visibly on its own. To confirm it is
still inert, grep a build log for "expected bun@": the warning is absent when
the range is satisfied.

Two things are deliberately left alone:

  - the model catalogue at models.opencode.ai, refreshed hourly. It is data,
    not code, and a stale baked-in catalogue would be worse than a fetch.
    OPENCODE_DISABLE_MODELS_FETCH=1 or OPENCODE_MODELS_PATH turn it off.

  - ripgrep. opencode downloads its own if `rg` is not on PATH, so the
    Requires: ripgrep in the spec removes the download entirely - which()
    searches PATH first and wins.

There is no telemetry, no Sentry and no analytics anywhere in the CLI.
OpenTelemetry exists but returns empty layers unless
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT is set.

Language servers and formatters are disabled by default upstream in this
version, so no configuration is shipped in /etc/opencode. That directory is
still read, and merged at the highest precedence of any file source, if an
administrator wants to put something there.


Licensing
---------

The dependency tree is compiled into the executable, so its licences are part
of the binary and the License: tag has to cover all of them. As of 1.18.32,
across the 511 shipped packages:

    MIT 292, Apache-2.0 116, ISC 75, BSD-2-Clause 9, BlueOak-1.0.0 9,
    BSD-3-Clause 6, 0BSD 1, CC0-1.0 1, CC-BY-3.0 1, CC-BY-4.0 1

No copyleft, nothing non-free. Two declarations are inconsistent and neither
matters: @npmcli/redact says ISC and ships MIT text, abbrev says ISC and its
LICENSE says "ISC OR MIT".

poe-oauth 0.0.8 needs a note, because it looks like a problem and is not. Its
package.json has no license field and the npm tarball has no LICENSE file,
which reads as all-rights-reserved. It is packages/poe-oauth of
github.com/poe-platform/poe-code, and that repository carries an MIT LICENSE
at its root. The omission is upstream's packaging, not the terms.

caniuse-lite is CC-BY-4.0 and requires attribution, so its LICENSE is
installed alongside the package's own. spdx-exceptions (CC-BY-3.0) and
spdx-license-ids (CC0-1.0) ship no licence text; both are lists of licence
identifiers, and the declaration in their package.json is the notice.

To recheck on a bump, read the license field of every
node_modules/.bun/*/node_modules/*/package.json in the vendor tarball and
diff the set against the list above. The keep list is a useful first look but
not a substitute: it names each entry as name@version, so an unchanged
opencode-vendor-keep.txt means the closure did not move at all and the counts
above must still hold - but a changed one only tells you where to look.


Native code
-----------

Everything compiled into the executable that is not JavaScript is built
from source, in three ways:

  - Loaded from a package at run time (like libopentui.so): libfff_c.so
    from the fff package (the file finder behind the @ file picker,
    OPENCODE_DISABLE_FFF falls back to an internal walk) and librust_pty.so
    from the bun-pty package (the pty behind the shell tool). %prep rewrites
    the two npm loaders to those literal paths and removes the npm binaries,
    so bun has nothing to embed. Both ABIs are unversioned and private to
    the TypeScript shipped here, hence the exact Requires.

  - Embedded, built here from source: @parcel/watcher's watcher.node from the
    C++ in its own npm package (binding.gyp's Linux branch, node-addon-api
    headers from Source11).

  - Embedded, taken from packages: the five grammar modules @opentui/core
    highlights with (javascript, typescript, markdown, markdown_inline, zig)
    are the tree-sitter-<lang>-wasm subpackages' files, copied over the
    asset directories before bun build. Those packages build every Factory
    grammar to WebAssembly with the %treesitter_wasm_* macros (clang, lld,
    wasi-libc through the tree-sitter CLI's own wasi-sdk invocation); the
    modules parse identically to upstream's release modules under the
    web-tree-sitter runtime opencode ships and accept opentui's highlight
    queries (checked when this was introduced, against javascript 0.23.1
    and markdown 0.5.3 where opentui pins 0.25.0 and 0.5.1).

  - Embedded, taken from a package: photon_rs_bg.wasm and its glue from
    photon-node (image resizing for vision models). The glue must be the one
    the same wasm-bindgen wrote, so the whole npm package directory is
    replaced by the package's files and upstream's two edits to the glue
    (patches/@silvia-odwyer%2Fphoton-node@*.patch: bundle under bun, read
    the module from where opencode embeds it) are re-applied by %prep.

The shell tool's command parser no longer goes through wasm at all:
opencode-native-tree-sitter.patch loads libtree-sitter and the
tree-sitter-bash / tree-sitter-powershell packages' grammars through
bun:ffi, with libopencode-tsshim.so (Source12, installed under %_libdir)
walking the tree, because TSNode is returned by value and bun:ffi cannot
receive that. %check parses a pipeline through the shipped shim.

One module is still the npm file: web-tree-sitter's tree-sitter.wasm
(205 KB, MIT), used by @opentui/core's highlighter. Its build needs
emscripten (MAIN_MODULE dynamic linking plus generated JavaScript glue),
which the distribution does not have. It is declared as
bundled(web-tree-sitter). Retiring it means either packaging emscripten or
giving the highlighter the same native treatment as the shell parser.

%check reads bun's embedded-file table out of the executable and asserts it
lists exactly watcher.node, the five grammar modules, photon's module and
tree-sitter.wasm, and that the files built here are in the payload byte
for byte. (Counting ELF headers does not work: bun's own executable
contains the magic three times.)


Things that will surprise you
-----------------------------

  - No debuginfo, on purpose. `bun build --compile` appends its payload past
    everything the ELF headers describe. eu-strip rewrites the file, drops
    the payload, and the result aborts at startup. Hence
    %global debug_package %{nil} and %global __strip /bin/true, and
    NO_BRP_STRIP_DEBUG=true in %install, because brp-15-strip-debug uses
    binutils strip on any ELF `file` calls "not stripped" - which the
    compiled binary is whenever the bun it was copied from kept its
    .symtab (a project built without debuginfo). The symptom is %check
    printing bun's version instead of opencode's.

  - The lockfile is regenerated rather than frozen. bun rewrites the key
    order of trustedDependencies and patchedDependencies, so any bun newer
    than the one upstream pins reports the lockfile as changed. When this was
    checked against 1.3.14 the only content difference was @vitest/coverage-v8
    and its closure - test tooling this build does not use.

  - --production does not work. @opencode-ai/core is a devDependency of
    packages/opencode and is compiled into the binary anyway.

  - bun --compile needs to find a .bun section in /proc/self/exe. Running bun
    through an explicit ld-linux wrapper breaks it with a confusing
    BunSectionNotFound.
